  "response": "Forensic engineers who must separate a design flaw from a construction defect cannot rely on either party's narrative. They reconstruct the structure twice: once as it was drawn and once as it was built. They then ask which version carries the failure. The Millbrook River pedestrian bridge shows how this method works under adversarial pressure. Ridgeway Engineering prepared the original design for the Millbrook River pedestrian bridge, and that design commission led to Castellan Builders serving as the general contractor for the bridge's construction. The bridge's main span collapsed during a load test in March 2021, and the two firms have offered irreconcilable explanations ever since.\n\nInvestigators begin by fixing the service history of the structure, because a span's loading record determines which failure mechanisms are plausible at all. The collapse occurred before the bridge was opened to the public; that is, the bridge had never carried public traffic at the time it collapsed. The record also places sustained use well ahead of the failure: the bridge had carried public foot traffic for five years before that March 2021 collapse. The casualty record is similarly contested, and investigators must settle it from site logs and medical documentation rather than from testimony. Either no workers were injured in the collapse, or two workers were killed in it. These accounts cannot both be true, and one of them must hold.\n\nWith the history established, analysts turn to the structure as designed. Investigators performed a finite element analysis of the bridge using the as-designed drawings. The finite element analysis indicated that the northern node's capacity was below its design load demand. This is the decisive diagnostic step, because a deficiency that appears in the idealized model cannot be attributed to the contractor. The northern truss node was under-designed for its load demand, though the capacity shortfall in the as-designed model does not by itself establish this. Field inspection of the wreckage, however, revealed a second deficiency. At least one of two conditions, perhaps both, set the stage for failure: the under-designed northern node, or the fact that the bolts at the truss joints were installed with insufficient tension. Forensic practice requires that each candidate be tested against the physical evidence rather than chosen by preference.\n\nThe dispute sharpened at this stage. The design firm argued that the contractor had installed undersized bolts at the truss joints. Investigators then examined the inspection records for precursor distress. Although the northern node was under-designed, no cracking at the truss nodes was documented before the collapse. An analyst must weigh that absence of documented distress carefully, since inspection intervals and access limitations can leave early damage unrecorded. The bolt allegation shaped the subsequent proceedings, and Ridgeway's argument about undersized bolts caused the arbitration panel to hold that the contractor's bolt installation did not materially contribute to the collapse. The arbitral finding narrowed the remaining question to the adequacy of the design itself.\n\nThat question was ultimately addressed by an independent technical body. The under-design of the northern truss node is borne out by the independent review board's conclusion that the design firm's calculations underestimated the load demand on the northern truss node. This convergence is what forensic engineers look for when parties dispute each other's accounts. The method combines a computational model built strictly from contract drawings, a physical examination of as-built conditions, and a review of the original calculations by an evaluator with no stake in the outcome. When all three lines of inquiry point to the same element, the attribution rests on reproducible engineering evidence rather than on the persuasiveness of either firm's advocacy. The construction irregularities then become secondary considerations, recorded for completeness but not treated as the governing cause.",
